NEW names the files this card creates; they and PATHS are this card's files, no others.
The contract is docs/STANDARD.md (embedded in AGENTS.md): read the sections a step names first; where this card and it disagree, it wins and the PR body says so.
Steps marked (hard) were dealt pro in the flat wave and may need pro; the card is dealt flash first and the machine escalates, never you.

THE TASK. A tree of 2 work steps (STEP 2 to STEP 3), walked in order. Each work step carries its own PATHS:, COMMIT: and VERDICT: lines: do it, run its check, commit only its PATHS with its COMMIT: line as the message, and write its verdict line in the PR body and RESULT, `step <n>: <ok|broken|not-done|skipped> <the full 40-hex commit sha pasted from git rev-parse HEAD, or - when nothing changed; a short sha is refused: e.g. step 2: ok 3b1f0c2a9d4e7f6a5c8b1d0e2f3a4b5c6d7e8f90 the change> <one line>` (ok with `-` and the commit that did it when the head already holds the step). The first step that is not ok stops the walk: steps before it land, and the coordinator turns the rest into a new card. Tests shrink through harnesses (the owner: \"the biggest win is to clean up larger tests into harnesses and structs and helper functions\", \"tiny modules that help make the tests themselves shorter, less code. less copy pasta\"): one constructor with defaults per unit and tests that change a few fields; a harness struct owning the plumbing and the cleanup; builders with scenario verbs; one checker per family; table-driven where cases differ only by data. For each package the step names: read every test file once and list the setup and checking code that repeats (three or more copies of one shape); put the package's pieces in the one new file the step names (a `rig` struct with a constructor taking `t`, its scenario methods, its checkers; one line of doc per method, each used by at least two shapes), using internal/testkit for the general mechanics (read internal/testkit/HARNESS.md); convert the one example file the step names to the rig; leave every other file to the shrink cards, which read the rig but never edit it. The PR body lists the shapes found (a count and an example file:line each) and the lines the rig should save.

HOW THIS CARD IS JUDGED. (1) Every behaviour a test pinned before is pinned after: count the checks (assert and require calls, table rows) before and after, and break three of the behaviours in the code on purpose to see the tests go red, then restore. (2) Production code changes only where a step says, and never its output. (3) No sleep, timer wait, socket, real clock or `t.Setenv` is added to a unit test; every test touched opens with `t.Parallel()`. (4) Test names are kept unless a step says otherwise (a Test that becomes a table row keeps its name as the row's). (5) Nothing outside PATHS and NEW.

