THE TASK (heavy: a state, its store and its model). Friend presence must not depend on the sprint server answering. Measured 2026-10-04 12:54 PM ET on the Studio: with the sprint machine STOPPED, POST /verbs on 127.0.0.1:6390 hangs (the client's 8 s timeout), every daemon's beat (`nova-sprint friend beat`, sent over internal/sprintwire) fails, emma's and johnny's last beat read 12:39 PM, and the friends table shows every friend silent although each daemon and session is alive. Presence is a building block of nova-tools: it lives in nova-friend, on the bus store, and a sprint is one of its readers.

The contract (write it into docs/SPEC-FRIEND.md as the section \"Presence\", the one place a reader learns it):
- One record per friend on the bus store: the hash `bus2:presence:<name>`, fields `name`, `seen` (RFC3339 UTC, milliseconds), `instance` (the daemon's instance id), `harness`, `route` (`push`, `defer` or `passive`: how a message reaches the open session now), `asleep` (0 or 1), `queue`, `working`, `width` (the numbers the daemon knows, 0 when none), `proved` (RFC3339 of the coordinator's last proved keepalive frame from this friend, empty when none) and `version`. The friend's daemon writes its own fields every second, one pipelined HSET plus PEXPIRE of 60 s, so a reader tells down (stale) from gone (no record, never ran or a minute dead); the coordinator's keepalive loop writes only `proved`.
- The state is a pure function of the record and a clock, `internal/friend` (one function, its rule cited from SPEC-FRIEND): `up` when `seen` is within DownAfter = 10 s and `asleep` is 0; `asleep` when within 10 s and `asleep` is 1; `down` when older than 10 s or the record is gone. At exactly 10 s it is down. A session the daemon has marked broken (fg-session-broken-on-provider-refusal: the provider refuses every turn) is `down` whatever `seen` says, with the fields `broken` (RFC3339 of the mark) and `reason` (the refusal line), so a reader never shows a broken friend up and working.
- The keepalive loop (internal/friend/keepalive, landed by fg-friend-keepalive-into-integration) loses its sprint dependency: its `Authority` is the coordinator's name from `--coordinator` (or the saved session state), with the seat generation an optional pass-through a sprint consumer may supply; its `HealthBatch` writes `proved` on the bus store. A sprint that wants a projection reads presence; nothing in nova-friend writes to a sprint.
- The daemon's sprint beat becomes optional and harmless: `--server` has no default; with none the daemon beats nothing to a sprint and its presence is whole; with one, the beat runs off the delivery loop (its own goroutine, its own deadline under one second), so a hung server never delays a delivery, a pong or a presence write, and its failure is one record line a minute, never a state change. `install` without `--server` writes an agent with none.
GENERAL: what this card adds imports no internal/sprint*, internal/nsprint* or internal/sprintwire package, says no sprint word in help or docs, and waits on no sprint server.
Unit tests use no real time and open no socket: an injected clock (or testing/synctest) and the package's fake store; the logic is a function apart from its transport (a server is a function of its input, a client takes a send function). Every new test opens with t.Parallel(), in testify. A test that needs Redis is functional (-tags functional) and runs only through tools/functionalrun --fresh-gocache --deadline 15m, never against a store on this machine.
Usable cold by an AI from the tool's help alone: the verb's help names every flag, every output line and JSON field, every exit code, and one example that runs as written; docs/CLI.md carries the same text; the verbhelp tests pin it.
Tests (red first): TestPresenceHoldsWhileTheSprintServerHangs (the daemon on a fake clock, its beat func blocking until the test ends: presence is written every second, deliveries and pongs go on, the record has one beat line a minute); TestPresenceIsDownAtTenSeconds (9.999 s up, 10.000 s down, asleep within 10 s, a missing record down); TestTheCoordinatorWritesOnlyProved; TestInstallWithoutServerWritesNoBeat. Functional (tools/functionalrun, the container's own store): one daemon writes `bus2:presence:<name>` within a second of start and the record is gone a minute after it stops.
TLA+ (states: up, asleep, down at 10 s): a module tla/Presence.tla with tla/MCPresence.cfg beside tla/Friend.tla. Variables: the record's seen and asleep, the clock, whether the daemon runs, whether the sprint server answers. Actions: Tick, Write (the daemon alive), Die, Sleep, Wake, ServerDown, ServerUp. Invariants: State = up exactly when now - seen < 10 and not asleep, whatever the server does; no action of the server changes presence. One broken config, tla/MCPresenceBrokenServerBeat.cfg, derives presence from the sprint beat and must fail the invariant. TLC on a small instance on a bench machine, never the Studio; rows in tla/RUNS.tsv and tla/CASES.tsv.

STEP 1. Set `JOB=~/rowan-working/jobs/<job>` from BRIEF.md, create only that directory, and clone inside it: `mkdir -p \"$JOB\" && cd \"$JOB\" && git clone -q https://github.com/mas-bandwidth/nova-tools.git repo && cd repo`. Create the exact STATUS branch from the current remote tip of origin/rowan/integration-2026-10-04. Export GOCACHE=~/rowan-working/.cache/go-build GOFLAGS=-mod=readonly NOVA_TEST_NO_HOST=1 before any go command. Read AGENTS.md and the governing spec section first; verify the defect or gap still exists at the tip before editing, and if it is already done, report HOLD with the exact evidence rather than duplicate it.

STEP 2. Do the task above, the red test first where there is code (the TEST line's name is the one to write; rename it in RESULT if the behaviour wants a better name).
  PATHS: internal/friend/**,cmd/nova-friend/main.go,cmd/nova-friend/main_test.go,cmd/nova-friend/verbhelp_test.go,internal/bus/bus.go,internal/bus/fake.go,internal/bus/redis.go,docs/SPEC-FRIEND.md,docs/CLI.md,docs/TESTS.md,tla/**
