THE TASK. nova-bus and nova-friend still lean on the sprint for their store. Measured at the base: cmd/nova-bus/main.go and cmd/nova-friend/main.go import internal/nsprint/redisauth; internal/bus/addr.go reads the fleet row's bus address from the sprint store at NOVA_SPRINT_REDIS (FleetBusKey) when neither --redis nor NOVA_BUS_REDIS names the store, and the login comes from NOVA_SPRINT_REDIS_USER and NOVA_SPRINT_REDIS_PASSWORD_ENV. A user with no sprint has none of these. Make the store general:
- The address: `--redis`, else `NOVA_BUS_REDIS`, else the fleet row's `bus` read from nova-config itself (internal/config, the rows `nova-config fleet set --bus` writes, through the DSN nova-config resolves), never from a sprint store; else the refusal, one line naming all three and the command that sets the row. The loopback-or-tailnet rule (internal/bus.CheckAddr) is unchanged and still runs before any dial.
- The login: `NOVA_BUS_REDIS_USER` names the user and `NOVA_BUS_REDIS_PASSWORD_ENV` the variable holding its password; the two NOVA_SPRINT_ names are read after them for this release, said in help as the old names.
- No import of internal/nsprint* or internal/sprint* from cmd/nova-bus, internal/bus or cmd/nova-friend: the login helper moves to (or is replaced by) a general package, internal/redisconn if it already holds the same thing.
If reading the row from nova-config is more than a small change at the tip, keep the flag and the variable, drop the sprint-store fallback, and say so in the PR (nothing guessed).
