      (:id "fg-delivery-pending-as-one-turn" :tier "-" :needs ("fg-delivery-no-progress-not-a-cap")
      :title "A message must not wait behind every older one"
      :brief "RESULT: fg-delivery-pending-as-one-turn sha=3f26f822126b tier: pro
REPO: mas-bandwidth/nova-tools
BASE: rowan/integration-2026-10-04
KIND: fix-red
DEPENDS-ON: fg-delivery-no-progress-not-a-cap
PATHS: internal/friend/daemon.go,internal/friend/daemon_test.go,internal/friend/adapter.go,internal/friend/adapter_test.go,internal/friend/machine.go,internal/friend/machine_test.go,docs/SPEC-FRIEND.md,docs/CLI.md
TEST: ./internal/friend TestATurnEndDeliversEveryPendingMessageAsOneTurnOldestFirst
Deadline: finish within 90 minutes.
You are a child of the coordinator: one task, one staged checkout, one branch, unattended. This card is the whole task. Read $JOB/JOB.md first. Start at the current BASE tip; admission inspected exact base 3f26f822126bda57b104b7049e9a3efa2d799560. Verify the gap still exists before editing; if it is already done, report not-done with exact evidence rather than duplicate work. One change, tests that are red before and green after. Write the draft early and commit it before any extra probe.
Libraries considered: the tree's own packages (internal/friend, internal/bus, the package's fake Exec) and testify; no new dependency. Counts toward nova-tools v1.2.0 (stream friends-general-v1-2-0). Glenn, 2026-10-04 ~1:00 PM ET: friends reliably talking with each other ship in nova-tools, through nova-bus and nova-friend, not opinionated only in nova-sprint; nova-sprint only consumes them. Every line this card adds passes one test: someone who never runs nova-sprint uses it as it is. A stranger with their own AIs must be able to do this from nova-tools and its docs alone; no Studio paths, names or our friends in the tool (Glenn, 2026-10-04 1:14 PM ET: 'everything we are doing inside nova-sprint and working with friends, other people can do too with their own AIs, using nova-tools ideally').

RULES.
Work only in the job directory this card names.
Never force-push or rebase a shared branch.
Never kill a process you did not start.
Never start a server on this machine.
No `rm -rf` outside the job directory.
Report what was not done.

THE TASK. A message must not wait behind every older one. Measured 2026-10-04 1:12 PM ET: Alex's daemon hands one bus message per turn and his turns run 2 to 10 minutes of real work, so each message waits for every older one; at 1:07 PM it delivered a \"coordinator silent since 12:37\" notice 30 minutes stale while the coordinator's 1:04 PM and 1:11 PM messages were still queued behind it. The rule:
- When the session is free (a turn has ended, or none ran), the daemon delivers ALL pending messages as ONE turn: one envelope, oldest first, each with its id, from, subject and age (`[1/3] <id> from=<f> at=<RFC3339> age=<m>m subject=<s>` then the body), capped at the adapter's text limit with the rest named by id (`and <n> more: nova-bus recv --as <me> --all`); every message in the envelope is acked when the turn is accepted (exit 0), none when it fails.
- Superseded notices are dropped, not delivered: the daemon's own notices of which a newer one exists (a \"coordinator silent\" followed by \"coordinator back\", or two silents) keep only the newest, and the dropped ones are acked with `superseded=<newer id>` in the record.
- A PING the daemon has answered (daemon-pong) is never pushed as a turn and is acked once answered, so a session's turns are spent on work. Up comes from the daemon's keepalive (the A4 contract, landed by fg-friend-keepalive-into-integration); the session proves life by any bus line it sends (a real message from it counts as its proof; `pong --nonce` still counts when a session runs it).
- One turn at a time, as fg-delivery-no-progress-not-a-cap (landed before this card) says.
The envelope and the supersede rule are pure functions over the pending list and a clock; the daemon is their shell.
GENERAL: what this card adds imports no internal/sprint*, internal/nsprint* or internal/sprintwire package, says no sprint word in help or docs, and waits on no sprint server.
Unit tests use no real time and open no socket: an injected clock (or testing/synctest) and the package's fake store; the logic is a function apart from its transport (a server is a function of its input, a client takes a send function). Every new test opens with t.Parallel(), in testify. A test that needs Redis is functional (-tags functional) and runs only through tools/functionalrun --fresh-gocache --deadline 15m, never against a store on this machine.
Usable cold by an AI from the tool's help alone: the verb's help names every flag, every output line and JSON field, every exit code, and one example that runs as written; docs/CLI.md carries the same text; the verbhelp tests pin it.
Tests, a fake Exec recording each turn's text and a fake clock: TestATurnEndDeliversEveryPendingMessageAsOneTurnOldestFirst (three messages queued during a 6-minute turn arrive as one next turn, oldest first, all acked on exit 0), TestAFailedEnvelopeAcksNothing, TestASupersededNoticeIsDroppedAndAckedWithItsSuccessor, TestAnAnsweredPingIsNeverATurn, TestAnyBusLineFromTheSessionIsItsProofOfLife, TestTheEnvelopeNamesWhatDidNotFit. SPEC-FRIEND: the loop section says the envelope, the supersede rule and that pings are the daemon's; CLI.md where status prints the envelope size. TLA+: the delivery model (tla/Delivery.tla, fg-session-broken-on-provider-refusal) gains the envelope: a turn takes the whole pending set; say in the PR which invariant covers it, or add one (no message delivered in a later turn than a younger one).

STEP 1. Enter the staged checkout JOB.md names, no clone; inspect git status and the actual full head. Work only on its own branch. Export GOCACHE=$JOB/cache/go-build GOFLAGS=-mod=readonly NOVA_TEST_NO_HOST=1 before any Go command. Scratch belongs under $JOB/scratch. Read AGENTS.md and the governing spec section; cite it from every function that implements a rule.
STEP 2. Write the red tests first, TestATurnEndDeliversEveryPendingMessageAsOneTurnOldestFirst among them, each opening with t.Parallel(). Run them and keep the failing lines as evidence.
STEP 3. Make them pass by changing only the named PATHS and NEW files, minimally. Commit the draft on your own branch as soon as the tests are green, before any further probe; a later commit may refine it. A change any other file needs goes in the report as a proposed diff, never a commit.
STEP 4. Run the gate: nice -n 19 go test -p 2 -count=1 -timeout 600s ./internal/friend/ ./cmd/nova-friend/ ./internal/ci/, and keep the last line of each. Run gofmt -l on every changed Go file; it must print nothing.
STEP 5. Inspect the diff and commit only the named files on your own branch. Present-tense comments and docs; no names of people, machines or friends. Commit trailer Co-Authored-By: Claude <your model> <noreply@anthropic.com>. Finish exactly through JOB.md's protocol, no direct external publication; the STEP3 SHA in RESULT.md is the exact 40-hex git rev-parse HEAD. Report the actual gates, the red-to-green evidence, the diff stat and deletions, every helper added, and what was not done. PR body ends with 🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code). Normal independent review is required before landing; never merge yourself.")))
