THE TASK. cmdQuarantine (cmd/nova-fuse/main.go:617-680), cmdLockdown (:542-615) and liftQuarantine (:369) are unlocked read-modify-write cycles (ReadBox then WriteBox), and internal/fuse/fuse.go note 3 admits two copies of the tool blowing fuses at once lose one write. The audit shows the worse half: the losing run has already printed QUARANTINE OK ... verified by re-reading the box and exited 0 when its entry is later overwritten by another writer's rename. Trigger: 24 concurrent quarantine --box box.json surf-N reason on one box: some exit 0 yet their entries are missing from the final box. Impact: a burst of parallel blows, the tool scenario, silently loses fuses it announced. Remedy: serialize the read-modify-write per box with internal/filelock (filelock.Lock(box+\".lock\", label, timeout)) held across ReadBox, the write and the verifying re-read in cmdQuarantine, liftQuarantine and cmdLockdown; for lockdown, if the lock cannot be taken within the timeout, blow anyway (a fuse you cannot blow is not a fuse) and say so on a NOTE line; update note 3 in internal/fuse/fuse.go. filelock never removes its file, so TestTheWriteLeavesNoLitter (main_test.go:535) must allow fuses.json.lock; a dry run must not create it. Test: in-process, 16 goroutines each run quarantine with a distinct surface against one box path; afterwards assert every run that returned 0 has its surface in the box; red before (flaky loss), green after repeated runs. (security#74 finding 3) The work lives in the files of the PATHS line, and those are the only files you may touch; a change any other file needs goes in the report as a proposed diff, never a commit. The test named on the TEST line is red before the change and green after.

