THE TASK. extractLinkTargets (internal/check/links.go:256-274) calls matchBrackets (links.go:280-294) at every '[' on a line, and matchBrackets scans forward to the end of the line when the brackets never close, so a line of N '[' costs O(N^2): one regular .md holding a single 200 KB line of '[' makes \"nova-check links\" take 5.6 s, 400 KB takes 22 s, a 4 MB line about 40 minutes; links has no deadline and quickstart runs links first, so a stranger's repository can hold a first run for minutes with an ordinary file. (spelling on the same file is linear.) Impact: CPU stall, no memory growth. Remedy: compute every bracket's matching ']' in one O(N) pass before the per-link loop: iterate the line once with a stack of '[' indexes, on ']' pop and record match[open] = i; leftover '[' have match -1; extractLinkTargets then reads match[i] instead of calling matchBrackets (delete matchBrackets or keep it only for tests). Behaviour must not change: nested badges (outer target still checked, see TestLinksBadgeOuterTargetChecked) and every existing links test stay green. Test TestExtractLinkTargetsIsLinearOnALineOfOpenBrackets: call extractLinkTargets on strings.Repeat(\"[\", 4_000_000) and assert it returns no targets; no clock is used, the red-before is that the quadratic scan runs for tens of minutes and hits the test timeout, green after in milliseconds; add small table cases proving nested and unclosed brackets give the same targets as before. (security#77 finding 5) The work lives in the files of the PATHS line, and those are the only files you may touch; a change any other file needs goes in the report as a proposed diff, never a commit. The test named on the TEST line is red before the change and green after.

