THE TASK. On darwin a symlink spelling of a granted path is unreadable inside the wall (security#67 finding 1). resolvePath (internal/sandbox/policy.go:447) returns only filepath.EvalSymlinks(raw), so Policy.Reads/Writes hold the resolved directory and DarwinProfile (internal/sandbox/profile.go:62-86) grants file-read* under the resolved READn/WRITEn only. Following a symlink needs read on the link itself; profiles/darwin.sb.tmpl grants that literal only for /etc /tmp /var (line ~101) and the xcode links. Trigger: nova-sandbox --write W --read /x/rlink -- cat /x/rlink/data.txt, rlink a symlink to directory r: 'Operation not permitted' while /x/r/data.txt reads; the same for a --write reached through a home symlink. The probe never uses a link spelling, so SANDBOX OK passes silently. Impact: availability only; no escape. Remedy: have Build record the caller's cleaned absolute spelling when it differs from the resolved path (new Policy field, e.g. LinkSpellings, checked with badPathText) and have DarwinProfile emit (allow file-read* (literal \"<spelling>\")) for it and for each symlink component of it, as the template does for /etc /tmp /var; add a sentence to rule 5 of docs/SPEC-SANDBOX.md. Test in internal/sandbox/policy_test.go: temp dir r plus symlink rlink to it, Build with --read rlink, DarwinProfile text must contain a literal grant naming the rlink spelling (red before: absent); a plain path emits no extra literal. The work lives in the files of the PATHS line, and those are the only files you may touch; a change any other file needs goes in the report as a proposed diff, never a commit. The test named on the TEST line is red before the change and green after.

