THE TASK. Nothing bounds the corpus read. Build reads each .md whole (internal/memindex/memindex.go:288, fs.ReadFile), copies it to a string for newline normalization, and builds per-chunk Terms maps plus corpus DF/Post maps sized by DISTINCT terms; verify re-reads files whole (verify.go:239, 271, 318, 374) and Coverage concatenates the whole B side into one builder (verify.go:243). Measured on one planted 40 MB file: stats peak RSS 460 MB (repeated words) to 2.06 GB (4.8M distinct tokens); search --channels trigram 3.17 GB; a 200 MB file extrapolates to 10-16 GB, on every indexing verb, and quickstart builds four times. Remedy: add exported constants in memindex (MaxFileBytes = 8 MiB, MaxCorpusBytes = 64 MiB, MaxVocabulary = 1_000_000); one helper readCapped(fsys, name) that Stats the opened file and reads through io.LimitReader(max+1), returning an error naming the file when over the cap, used at memindex.go:288 and the four verify.go reads; Build also returns an error naming the cap when the running byte total passes MaxCorpusBytes or len(c.DF) passes MaxVocabulary. State the three numbers in docs/CLI.md in the nova-memory section. Test TestBuildRefusesAFileOverTheByteCap: fstest.MapFS with a.md of MaxFileBytes+1 bytes makes Build return an error containing \"a.md\" and \"8388608\"; a file of exactly MaxFileBytes builds (red before: no cap exists, so the const does not compile / the error is nil). (security#76 finding 3) The work lives in the files of the PATHS line, and those are the only files you may touch; a change any other file needs goes in the report as a proposed diff, never a commit. The test named on the TEST line is red before the change and green after.

