THE TASK. A tree of 2 work steps (STEP 2 to STEP 3), walked in order. Each work step carries its own PATHS:, COMMIT: and VERDICT: lines: do it, run its check, commit only its PATHS with its COMMIT: line as the message, and write its verdict line in the PR body and RESULT, `step <n>: <ok|broken|not-done|skipped> <the full 40-hex commit sha pasted from git rev-parse HEAD, or - when nothing changed; a short sha is refused: e.g. step 2: ok 3b1f0c2a9d4e7f6a5c8b1d0e2f3a4b5c6d7e8f90 the change> <one line>` (ok with `-` and the commit that did it when the head already holds the step). The first step that is not ok stops the walk: steps before it land, and the coordinator turns the rest into a new card. Every tool on the skeleton contract: one `testkit.Contract` test per tool (internal/testkit: it probes the bare command, an unknown verb, an unknown flag per verb, two bad flags in one run, --json on each verb's refusal, `<verb> -h`, and a remedy run back through the tool), and each breach it names fixed in the tool's verb declarations or refusals; a breach that needs a change outside PATHS is named in the PR body. The gate's update lowers the tool-answers rows the fixes clear.

HOW THIS CARD IS JUDGED. (1) Each step's change is the one the step names and no other: every +/- line of `git diff -U0` traces to one sentence of its step. (2) Behaviour no step names is unchanged: the package's existing tests pass unedited, except an expected line a step itself changes. (3) A new behaviour has a test that fails before the change and passes after (write it first, see it red), named for what it pins, opening with `t.Parallel()`, in testify. (4) A printed event prefix or field name that docs/TESTS.md shows is kept: add a field, never rename one, unless the step says so. (5) A refusal names what the input wants and the command to run next, in the skeleton's grammar (`VERB REFUSED: <why>; run: <remedy>`). (6) Nothing is guessed: where the code cannot tell what is right, the PR body says so and the step ends partial.

