THE TASK. nova-tools v1.2.0, lens stranger: a real cold run by a bud from the README and help alone. The goal: run a nova-friend for a made-up friend whose harness is a fake you write (the smallest program that reads the delivered job and writes the outbox report the help says it must; write it in Go on the bench, never a shell script, and if the help only shows a shell example that is a stumble), deliver it one job over nova-bus on a throwaway Redis inside the container, and see the report come back, as a person new to nova-tools would. Rules of a cold run: you read only nova-tools README.md (and the README files it links) and the tools' own help (`<tool> help`, `<tool> <verb> -h`); never the source, the specs, docs/CLI.md or any other doc, and never your memory of the project; when you cannot go on from those alone, that is a stumble, and you then read whatever you need to get past it and say what you read. Work on a Linux bench (vision, else hetzner), inside ONE throwaway container you run there for the whole session from the functional image (infra/functional-image, which carries Redis): `podman run --rm --timeout 14400 --network none` with ~/nova-bench/stranger/<job>/ mounted as its work directory, so every process of the run, the Redis included, belongs to the container runtime and dies with it (the reason a bare server is refused: nobody would stop it). Inside it, install the binaries exactly as README.md says (if it says to build, build there) and bring up the Redis the README or help tells you to, on loopback inside the container; never the fleet stores, never the Studio. When you finish, the container is gone and you remove ~/nova-bench/stranger/<job>/. A stumble is any moment you had to guess, retry, or read something other than README and help, and any output that misled you. Record the run in the file PATHS names: a Setup section (bench, versions printed by `<tool> version`), a Transcript section (every command you ran and its exact output, trimmed only